<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">André-Marie Ampère</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ampère</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">André-Marie Ampère was a French physicist and mathematician, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, and one of the founders of classical electromagnetism, which he called electrodynamics. He educated himself in his father's library, lost his father to the guillotine in 1793 during the Jacobin purges and later taught at the École polytechnique and the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hans-christian-rsted">Hans Christian Ørsted</a>'s discovery that an electric current deflects a magnetic needle was shown in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in September 1820, Ampère showed that two parallel wires carrying currents attract or repel each other. He coined the term solenoid, and the SI unit of electric current, the ampere, is named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/andre-marie-ampere.jpg" alt="André-Marie Ampère" width="330" height="446" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ambroise Tardieu · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ampere_Andre_1825.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ampère was born on 20 January 1775 to Jean-Jacques Ampère, a prosperous businessman, and Jeanne Antoinette Desutières-Sarcey. He grew up at the family property at Poleymieux-au-Mont-d'Or near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a>, now the Ampère Museum. His father admired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-rousseau">Jean-Jacques Rousseau</a>'s theories of education and let his son teach himself in a well-stocked library, with Buffon's Histoire naturelle and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Diderot</a> and d'Alembert as his schoolmasters. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> so as to read <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonhard-euler">Leonhard Euler</a> and Daniel Bernoulli and began teaching himself advanced mathematics at the age of twelve. His mother was a devout Catholic. His father, a local judge under the revolutionary government, was guillotined on 24 November 1793.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ampère married Julie Carron in 1799, took his first regular job as a mathematics teacher that year and had a son, Jean-Jacques, who became a scholar of languages. In 1802 he became professor of physics and chemistry at the École Centrale in Bourg-en-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bresse">Bresse</a> and wrote a treatise on probability. His wife died in July 1803. He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and began tutoring at the École Polytechnique in 1804, and despite lacking formal qualifications became professor of mathematics there in 1809. He joined the mathematicians of the Institut Impérial in 1814 and in 1824 was elected to the chair of experimental physics at the Collège de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><p>In September 1820 François Arago showed the Academy of Sciences Hans Christian Ørsted's discovery that a magnetic needle is deflected by an adjacent electric current. Ampère showed that two parallel wires carrying currents attract or repel each other, depending on whether the currents flow in the same or opposite directions, and so laid the foundation of electrodynamics. He generalised the results in the principle later called Ampère's law and theorised an electrodynamic molecule, a forerunner of the idea of the electron. In 1826 he published his magnum opus on the mathematical theory of electrodynamic phenomena, uniquely deduced from experience, which named the new science.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ampère died on 10 June 1836. He was elected a Foreign Member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-society">Royal Society</a> in 1827, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-clerk-maxwell">James Clerk Maxwell</a> called him the Newton of electricity. The Einstein–de Haas effect of 1915 supported his hypothesis. An international convention signed at the 1881 International Exposition of Electricity established the ampere as a standard unit of electrical measurement. His name is one of the 72 inscribed on the Eiffel Tower, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> metro station, a lunar mountain and an asteroid bear it. He also gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-language">English language</a> the word kinematic, from his cinématique, and coined the term solenoid.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “André-Marie Ampère” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
